sections.body:
Every Node project intended to be run, shared, or published has a package.json file at its root -- it's the manifest that tells both npm and other developers what the project is and needs. Running npm init in an empty directory generates a starting one interactively; in practice most developers just write or copy one directly. The fields that matter for almost every project:

```json
{
  "name": "order-service",
  "version": "1.4.0",
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node index.js",
    "dev": "node --watch index.js",
    "test": "node --test"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "express": "^4.18.2"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "eslint": "^8.50.0"
  }
}
```

name and version identify the package (required if you ever plan to publish it, but conventional even for private apps that never will be). main points at the entry-point file that other code gets when it requires or imports this package by name (less relevant for an app you just run directly, essential for a package meant to be depended on by others). scripts defines named shell commands runnable via npm run <name> (start and test get a small shortcut: npm start and npm test, no run needed) -- this is where most projects hide their actual build/lint/test/dev-server commands behind memorable names, rather than requiring every contributor to remember the full underlying command. dependencies and devDependencies are both installed locally by default, but are meant to be treated very differently, covered further down.

It's tempting to build a file path by just concatenating strings with a slash: __dirname + '/data/' + filename. This works fine on macOS and Linux, and then breaks in subtle ways the moment someone runs the exact same code on Windows, where the native path separator is a backslash, not a forward slash -- and even on POSIX systems, naive concatenation can produce paths with doubled slashes or unresolved '..' segments that some tools handle inconsistently. The path module exists specifically so you never have to think about the current OS's separator convention yourself.

path.join(...segments) concatenates path segments using the correct separator for the current platform, and normalizes the result (collapsing redundant slashes, resolving '.'/'..' segments) -- but it does not make the result absolute if the inputs weren't already. path.resolve(...segments) is the one that guarantees an absolute path: it processes segments from right to left, treating each one as relative to the previous, until it hits one that's already absolute (or runs out, in which case it falls back to the current working directory) -- effectively simulating 'if you cd'd through these segments one by one from here, where would you end up.'

The single most common practical use of path.join is combining it with __dirname (in CommonJS) or the reconstructed equivalent (in ESM) to build a path relative to the *current source file's own location*, rather than relative to wherever the process happens to be running from:

```javascript
const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

// WRONG: './data/report.json' resolves against process.cwd(),
// which is wherever the process was LAUNCHED from -- not where this file lives
const brittle = await fs.readFile('./data/report.json', 'utf8');

// RIGHT: always resolves relative to this specific source file, regardless
// of what directory someone happened to run `node ...` from
const reliable = await fs.readFile(
  path.join(__dirname, 'data', 'report.json'),
  'utf8'
);
```

That first version working perfectly on your machine, and then failing with an ENOENT ('no such file or directory') error the moment a teammate runs the exact same script from a different directory, is an extremely common real bug, and it's worth internalizing exactly why: a relative path string on its own carries no information about the source file it's written in -- it's always resolved against process.cwd(), the process's current working directory at the moment it started, which has nothing to do with your file structure unless you happen to always launch node from the same place.

The http core module is what actually implements HTTP in Node -- Express, and every other Node web framework, is ultimately a layer of convenience built directly on top of it. http.createServer((req, res) => { ... }) takes a handler function that gets called once per incoming request, with two objects: req (the incoming request, a readable stream, carrying .url, .method, .headers) and res (the outgoing response, a writable stream, which you control directly).

A detail that surprises people coming from a framework first: **you must explicitly call res.end()** to complete the response -- there's no automatic 'the handler function returned, so send whatever we have.' Forget it, and the client's connection simply hangs, waiting, until it eventually times out -- Node has no way to know you're 'done' with a response other than being told so explicitly, because res is a stream, and streams don't close themselves.

Reading a request body surfaces the same stream-based nature even more sharply. Unlike a framework, where req.body is typically just sitting there ready to read, the raw http module gives you req as a stream of raw data chunks (Buffers) that arrive over time, asynchronously, as the client sends them -- there is no synchronous 'get me the whole body' call, because the whole body might not have even arrived yet by the time your handler function starts running. Collecting it requires listening for 'data' events (each one delivering another chunk) and doing the actual work only once the 'end' event fires, signaling the client has finished sending:

```javascript
function readBody(req) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks = [];
    req.on('data', (chunk) => chunks.push(chunk));
    req.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8')));
    req.on('error', reject);
  });
}
```

This is precisely the kind of boilerplate that a body-parsing middleware (like Express's express.json()) exists to automate away -- but understanding that it's automating *this specific, genuinely non-trivial piece of work*, rather than magic, is exactly the point of building it once by hand.

The http module has no built-in concept of 'routes' at all -- every single request, regardless of URL or method, invokes the exact same handler function you gave to createServer. Building even a small API means manually branching on req.method and req.url yourself:

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/health') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok' }));
  } else if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/users') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ users: ['Amy', 'Ben'] }));
  } else {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Not found' }));
  }
});

server.listen(3000);
```

This works, but notice how much is already being hand-rolled for just two trivial routes: matching method AND path together for every branch, remembering the Content-Type header on every response, an explicit fallback for anything unmatched (skip that, and an unrecognized URL would fall through with no response at all, hanging the client), and nothing yet for parsing query strings, extracting path parameters like an :id segment, parsing a JSON request body (the readBody helper above would need to be called and its result JSON.parsed, by hand, in every route that needs it), or catching a thrown error inside a route handler so one bad request doesn't take down the whole server. Every one of those needs grows roughly linearly with the number of routes if hand-rolled directly -- which is exactly the gap a framework like Express is built to close, and exactly why the next concept picks up from here rather than starting from scratch.

examples.code:
const fs = require('fs');
const fsPromises = require('fs/promises');

// Synchronous -- fine here, since this runs once at startup, before serving any traffic
const configSync = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('./config.json', 'utf8'));

// Callback-based -- non-blocking, error-first convention
fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  const configCb = JSON.parse(data);
  console.log(configCb);
});

// Promise-based -- non-blocking, pairs with async/await
async function loadConfig() {
  const data = await fsPromises.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8');
  return JSON.parse(data);
}

const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

// Breaks the moment `node app.js` is run from a different working directory
async function brittleLoad() {
  return fs.readFile('./data/report.json', 'utf8');
}

// Always resolves relative to THIS source file's own location, regardless of cwd
async function reliableLoad() {
  const fullPath = path.join(__dirname, 'data', 'report.json');
  return fs.readFile(fullPath, 'utf8');
}

const http = require('http');

function readBody(req) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks = [];
    req.on('data', (chunk) => chunks.push(chunk));
    req.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks).toString('utf8')));
    req.on('error', reject);
  });
}

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');

  if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/health') {
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ status: 'ok' }));
    return;
  }

  if (req.method === 'POST' && req.url === '/echo') {
    const rawBody = await readBody(req);
    const parsed = JSON.parse(rawBody);
    res.statusCode = 200;
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ youSent: parsed }));
    return;
  }

  res.statusCode = 404;
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ error: 'Not found' }));
});

server.listen(3000);

