sections.body:
Every middleware function receives a third argument, conventionally named next, which is itself a function. Calling next() hands control forward to the next matching middleware in the stack -- Express doesn't move on by itself just because your function returned; it only moves on because you explicitly told it to. This single mechanism is the entire engine behind the pipeline:

```javascript
function logger(req, res, next) {
  console.log(`${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();   // hand off to whatever comes next in the stack
}
```

Getting this wrong produces two distinct, common bugs. **Not calling next() (and not sending a response either)** leaves the request permanently stuck -- Express is still waiting for that middleware to decide what happens next, and since nothing ever tells it, the client's connection just hangs until it times out, exactly like the missing res.end() bug from the raw http module, just one layer up. **Calling next() after already sending a response** (or calling it twice) produces a runtime error/warning, because Express expects exactly one decision per request at each step: either you finish the response yourself (res.send(), res.json(), res.end()), or you hand off to the next middleware with next() -- not both, and not neither. There's also a special case worth knowing early: calling next(someError) -- passing an argument -- tells Express this middleware failed, and instead of proceeding to the next *normal* middleware in line, Express skips straight ahead to the nearest error-handling middleware (covered in the last section).

Middleware run in exactly the order they were registered with app.use/app.get/etc -- not some inferred priority, not alphabetical, not 'security-related things first.' This has very real consequences. An authentication check meant to protect a route must be registered *before* that route's own handler in the source code, not after:

```javascript
// BROKEN: the route handler runs and responds BEFORE the auth check ever gets a turn
app.get('/admin/dashboard', dashboardHandler);
app.use(requireAuth);

// FIXED: auth check runs first; only calls next() (letting the request continue)
// if the request is actually authenticated
app.use(requireAuth);
app.get('/admin/dashboard', dashboardHandler);
```

In the broken version, an unauthenticated request to /admin/dashboard matches dashboardHandler first, simply because it was registered earlier in the stack -- by the time requireAuth would run, the response has often already been sent, and the check never meaningfully protects anything. The same ordering rule explains why body-parsing middleware like express.json() has to be registered before any route handler that reads req.body: if a route handler runs first in the stack, req.body won't have been populated yet, since the middleware that would have populated it hasn't had its turn.

Express identifies **error-handling middleware** specifically by its function *arity* -- four parameters, (err, req, res, next), instead of the usual three. This has a subtle but important consequence for ordering: an error middleware only catches errors from middleware registered *before* it in the stack, since Express walks the stack in registration order and only consults later entries once something earlier calls next(err). This is exactly why error-handling middleware is conventionally registered dead last, after every normal route and middleware -- registering it earlier would mean it simply never sees errors from routes defined after it.

examples.code:
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log(`${new Date().toISOString()} ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
  next();
});

app.get('/health', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ status: 'ok' });
});

app.post('/users', (req, res) => {
  const { name } = req.body;
  res.status(201).json({ id: 1, name });
});

app.listen(3000);

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  if (!req.headers.authorization) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  }
  next();
}

// BROKEN: unauthenticated requests reach the handler and get real data back
app.get('/admin/dashboard', (req, res) => res.json({ secrets: 'yes' }));
app.use(requireAuth);

// FIXED: requireAuth runs first and can short-circuit before the handler ever runs
app.use(requireAuth);
app.get('/admin/dashboard', (req, res) => res.json({ secrets: 'yes' }));

app.get('/risky', (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = 10 / getUntrustedDivisor();   // may throw
    res.json({ result });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);   // hands off to error-handling middleware instead of crashing
  }
});

// Registered LAST, after every other route and middleware
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error('Unhandled error:', err.message);
  res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong' });
});

